# Components, JSX, and Props

React apps are trees of components. A component is just a function that returns JSX (HTML-like syntax embedded in JavaScript).

```jsx
function Greeting({ name }) {
  return <h1>Hello, {name}!</h1>;
}

function App() {
  return <Greeting name="Ada" />;
}
```

## Key ideas
- Props are read-only inputs passed from parent to child — never mutate `props` inside a component.
- JSX expressions use `{}` to embed JavaScript values.
- Component names must start with a capital letter so React can distinguish them from HTML tags.

**Key takeaways**
- Think in components: break the UI into small, reusable pieces.
- Data flows down via props; behavior flows up via callback props.

# useState and Re-renders

```jsx
import { useState } from "react";

function Counter() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  return (
    <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>
      Clicked {count} times
    </button>
  );
}
```

## How it works
- Calling `setCount` schedules a re-render with the new value.
- State updates are asynchronous and batched — don't rely on `count` being updated immediately after calling `setCount`.
- Use the functional updater form (`setCount(c => c + 1)`) when the new state depends on the previous state.

**Key takeaways**
- Each component instance has its own isolated state.
- Never mutate state directly (e.g. `count++`) — always call the setter.

# useEffect for Side Effects

```jsx
import { useState, useEffect } from "react";

function Profile({ userId }) {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    fetch(`/api/users/${userId}`)
      .then(res => res.json())
      .then(setUser);
  }, [userId]); // re-run only when userId changes

  return user ? <p>{user.name}</p> : <p>Loading...</p>;
}
```

## Key takeaways
- The dependency array controls when the effect re-runs; an empty array `[]` means "run once on mount".
- Return a cleanup function from `useEffect` to cancel subscriptions or timers when the component unmounts.

# Client-Side Routing with React Router

```jsx
import { BrowserRouter, Routes, Route, Link } from "react-router-dom";

function App() {
  return (
    <BrowserRouter>
      <nav>
        <Link to="/">Home</Link>
        <Link to="/about">About</Link>
      </nav>
      <Routes>
        <Route path="/" element={<Home />} />
        <Route path="/about" element={<About />} />
        <Route path="/users/:id" element={<UserProfile />} />
      </Routes>
    </BrowserRouter>
  );
}
```

## Reading route params
```jsx
import { useParams } from "react-router-dom";
function UserProfile() {
  const { id } = useParams();
  return <p>User {id}</p>;
}
```

**Key takeaways**
- `Link` prevents full page reloads, unlike a plain `<a>` tag.
- Dynamic segments (`:id`) are read with `useParams()`.

# Fetching Data and Handling States

```jsx
function Posts() {
  const [posts, setPosts] = useState([]);
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);
  const [error, setError] = useState(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    fetch("/api/posts")
      .then(res => {
        if (!res.ok) throw new Error("Failed to load");
        return res.json();
      })
      .then(setPosts)
      .catch(setError)
      .finally(() => setLoading(false));
  }, []);

  if (loading) return <p>Loading...</p>;
  if (error) return <p>Error: {error.message}</p>;
  return <ul>{posts.map(p => <li key={p.id}>{p.title}</li>)}</ul>;
}
```

**Key takeaways**
- Always model three states explicitly: loading, error, success.
- Use a stable `key` prop (like an id) when rendering lists — never the array index if the list can reorder.

# Why TanStack Query Over Raw Fetch

Hand-rolled fetch logic re-implements the same things on every component: caching, retries, deduping requests, background refetching.

```jsx
import { useQuery } from "@tanstack/react-query";

function Posts() {
  const { data, isLoading, error } = useQuery({
    queryKey: ["posts"],
    queryFn: () => fetch("/api/posts").then(r => r.json()),
  });
  if (isLoading) return <p>Loading...</p>;
  if (error) return <p>Error!</p>;
  return <ul>{data.map(p => <li key={p.id}>{p.title}</li>)}</ul>;
}
```

**Key takeaways**
- `queryKey` uniquely identifies cached data — components requesting the same key share a cache entry.
- Server state (API data) behaves differently from client state and benefits from a dedicated library.

# Code Splitting and Performance

```jsx
import { lazy, Suspense } from "react";

const Dashboard = lazy(() => import("./Dashboard"));

function App() {
  return (
    <Suspense fallback={<p>Loading...</p>}>
      <Dashboard />
    </Suspense>
  );
}
```

## Key takeaways
- `lazy` + `Suspense` splits your bundle so users only download code for the route they visit.
- Measure before optimizing — use browser devtools' Lighthouse/Performance tab to find real bottlenecks rather than guessing.

# Core Components and Styling

```jsx
import { View, Text, StyleSheet } from "react-native";

function Card() {
  return (
    <View style={styles.card}>
      <Text style={styles.title}>Hello React Native</Text>
    </View>
  );
}

const styles = StyleSheet.create({
  card: { padding: 16, borderRadius: 8, backgroundColor: "#fff" },
  title: { fontSize: 18, fontWeight: "600" },
});
```

## Key differences from web React
- No `<div>` or `<span>` — use `View` and `Text` instead; all text must be wrapped in `<Text>`.
- Styling uses `StyleSheet.create` (a JS object, not CSS) with a Flexbox-based layout by default.
- Components render to real native UI elements (UIView on iOS, android.view on Android), not a WebView.

**Key takeaways**
- Layout defaults to Flexbox with `flexDirection: "column"`, opposite of the web's default `row`.
- Text content must always be inside a `<Text>` component — raw strings inside `<View>` throw an error.

# Stack and Tab Navigation

```jsx
import { NavigationContainer } from "@react-navigation/native";
import { createNativeStackNavigator } from "@react-navigation/native-stack";

const Stack = createNativeStackNavigator();

function App() {
  return (
    <NavigationContainer>
      <Stack.Navigator>
        <Stack.Screen name="Home" component={HomeScreen} />
        <Stack.Screen name="Details" component={DetailsScreen} />
      </Stack.Navigator>
    </NavigationContainer>
  );
}

// Inside HomeScreen
navigation.navigate("Details", { itemId: 42 });
```

## Key takeaways
- Stack navigators push/pop screens like a call stack — natural for drill-down flows.
- Tab navigators (`createBottomTabNavigator`) suit top-level sections a user jumps between freely.
- Pass data between screens via route params, read on the destination with `route.params`.

# Permissions and Device APIs

```jsx
import * as Location from "expo-location";

async function getLocation() {
  const { status } = await Location.requestForegroundPermissionsAsync();
  if (status !== "granted") return null;
  return await Location.getCurrentPositionAsync({});
}
```

## Key concepts
- Sensitive APIs (camera, location, contacts) require explicit runtime permission requests on both iOS and Android.
- Always handle the denied-permission case gracefully — don't assume the user will grant it.
- Libraries like Expo wrap native modules so you write JavaScript instead of native Swift/Kotlin for common device features.

**Key takeaways**
- Request permissions only when the feature is actually needed (just-in-time), not all at app launch.
- Design a clear fallback UI for when permission is denied, rather than a silent failure.

# Local Storage and Offline Data

```jsx
import AsyncStorage from "@react-native-async-storage/async-storage";

await AsyncStorage.setItem("draft", JSON.stringify(formData));
const saved = JSON.parse(await AsyncStorage.getItem("draft"));
```

## Key takeaways
- `AsyncStorage` is a simple key-value store suited for small amounts of data (settings, drafts) — not a replacement for a real database.
- For structured, larger offline datasets, a local database (e.g. SQLite via `expo-sqlite`) is more appropriate.
- Mobile networks are unreliable; design flows that tolerate offline use and sync gracefully when connectivity returns.
